  "step_2": "The individual states that the two lotteries are almost identical in terms of amounts and chances. The individual notes that in Lottery B, one can win more (2500 Euros) with almost the same chance (33%). This indicates that the individual focused on the magnitude of the prize and the slight difference in probability, emphasizing the higher potential gain in Lottery B. The individual does not mention anything about probability distribution, skewness, or the relative weighting of favorable versus unfavorable outcomes. There is no reference to the structure of the probability mass or any evaluation of how the probabilities are distributed across outcomes. The justification is based on the size of the prize and the similarity of chances, not on skewness or distributional asymmetry.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) — specifically, the distribution of probability mass on the favorable outcome — is not considered by the individual. The individual focuses only on the higher prize amount in Lottery B and the near-identical probability, without analyzing the relative weight of the favorable outcome. The decision reason requires evaluating whether one lottery has more probability assigned to the favorable outcome (i.e., left-skewness), which would make it preferable. However, the individual does not mention this structural feature. Furthermore, the individual’s justification centers on the prize amount, not on skewness. Therefore, the decision reason was not used in the deliberation process. The individual did not consider the skewness of the lotteries or apply the rule that prefers the more left-skewed one.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
